Texture: bound a texture's mipmap count, which is quadratic work read off the file.

`Texture::load` (`src/Texture.cc`) reads `mipmapCount` straight from the stream
and then walks one iteration per level:

	this->_m_mipmap_count = std::max(1u, r->read_uint());
	...
	for (std::int64_t level = this->_m_mipmap_count - 1; level >= 0; --level) {
		auto size = _ztex_mipmap_size(format, width, height, level);
		...
	}

Nothing bounds the count, and `_ztex_mipmap_size` halves the dimensions *inside*
its own loop, once per level:

	for (uint32_t i = 0; i < level; i++) {
		if (x > 1) x >>= 1;
		if (y > 1) y >>= 1;
	}

So loading one texture costs work quadratic in a number the file supplies. It is
not an allocation blow-up and not an out-of-bounds access — nothing throws and
nothing is corrupted, the reader simply never comes back. That is what makes it
hard to tell apart from patch `0027`: `0027` fixed a spin at 100% CPU with the
cursor parked at the end of the archive, and this one looks identical from
outside while being a different defect entirely.

Measured against our synthetic BinSafe fixture (`test/fixtures/minimal.g2.zen`,
4211 bytes): one byte at file offset 679 — byte 2 of the first shared lightmap
texture's `mipmapCount`, inside the mesh's LIGHTMAPS_SHARED (`0xB026`) chunk —
turns 1 into 9,568,257, which is on the order of 9e13 halvings. Found by
`tools/fuzz-world.js --seed 17`, the last of the 40-seed run still failing after
patch `0031`, and delta-debugged from 20 mutations down to that one byte.

The fix is a bound and nothing else. `width` and `height` are `uint32`, so a
mipmap chain cannot need more than 32 halvings to reach 1x1 and no well-formed
texture can reach the limit — the check is only ever hit by a file whose texture
header is already nonsense.

`ParserError` derives from `zenkit::Error`, so this reports as a normal parse
failure through the same path a truncated archive already takes.

Upstreamable as-is: a plain robustness bug, no API change, one guard.

